  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1135 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nA tight pressure gradient is expected on Saturday as the region\nwill be in between a departing high across the northeast and an \napproaching low developing over the northern Plains. This will\ncreate gusty southerly winds across Wisconsin, allowing for robust\nwarm air advection. Winds are expected to gust to 30-35 mph \nduring the day as daytime highs soar into the lower 80s away from \nthe lakeshore. Closer to the lake highs are only expected to rise \ninto the lower 70s. Winds will subsequently weaken Saturday night\nas the atmosphere decouples with a looser pressure gradient\nleading to calmer winds during the day on Sunday. Despite less\nwindy conditions on Sunday, highs will be similar to Saturday.\nHighs this weekend could approach or even break record highs both\ndays.\n\nPrecipitation chances early next week look to be delayed a bit as\nthe main energy from an approaching cold front looks to swing\nthrough Monday night instead of Monday afternoon and evening. This\nmeans relatively low (10-20%) chances for rain Monday afternoon\nwith a better chance (60-70%) Monday evening into the overnight\nhours. Despite the relatively high chance for rain, the amount of\nrain that is expected to fall is fairly minimal. The probability\nfor 0.1 inches or greater has risen a bit to 40-50% but not enough\nthat the QPF should be appreciably higher.\n\nAnother more widespread chance for precipitation may comes during\nthe end of next week as global ensembles show a deepening low \npressure system lifting across the western Great Lakes. Still a \nlot of uncertainty with the details of this system, but this looks\nlike the best chance for a soaking rain over the next 7-10 days.",
